<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hubal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hubal was a god worshipped in pre-Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, whose statue of red stone with a golden hand stood inside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>. Divination arrows were cast before it to settle disputes and decisions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> invoked him in their wars against Muhammad, and the image was destroyed when the Muslims took Mecca in 630.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hubal.jpg" alt="Hubal" width="212" height="281" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AimanAbir18plus · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hubal,_pre-Islamic_Arabian_god.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Hubal was a god worshipped in pre-Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, notably by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. His icon was a human figure believed to control divination. According to Hisham Ibn al-Kalbi's Book of Idols the image was shaped like a human, with the right hand broken off and replaced by a golden hand, and made of red agate; the early Islamic commentator al-Azraqi gave its material as cornelian pearl. Karen Armstrong writes that the sanctuary was dedicated to Hubal, who was worshipped as the chief of the 360 idols in the Kaaba, which probably represented the days of the year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Seven arrows were placed before the image and cast for divination in cases of death, virginity and marriage, and the direction in which they pointed answered the questions asked. Al-Azraqi adds that the image had a vault for sacrifice, with an offering of a hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>. In a tale recorded by Ibn al-Kalbi, Muhammad's grandfather Abdul Mutallib vowed to sacrifice one of his ten children and consulted the arrows, which pointed to his son Abd-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, the future father of Muhammad; the son was saved when 100 camels were sacrificed in his place.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> were defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-badr">Battle of Badr</a>, their leader Abu Sufyan ibn Harb is said to have called on Hubal for support in the next battle, saying &quot;Show your superiority, Hubal&quot;. When Muhammad conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 630, he broke the statue of Hubal along with the other 360 images and dedicated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name may derive from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>, possibly from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> form meaning &quot;he is Baal&quot;, and both were depicted with a missing or broken right hand. Ibn al-Kalbi says the image was first set up by Khuzayma ibn Mudrika, while Ibn Ishaq holds that Amr ibn Luhayy, a leader of the Khuza'a tribe, put it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> later became protectors of the holy place in place of the Khuza'a. Al-Azraqi says the image was brought from Hit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</p><p>Outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a> the name appears only once, in a Nabataean inscription beside the gods Dushara and Manawatu. Evidence is so slight that his role is hard to establish: scholars have called him a warrior and rain god, and the older view that he was a lunar deity has been rejected.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hubal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kaaba housed images of many deities, <b>Hubal</b> chief among them, and Allah was recognised as a high god.</blockquote><a href="/state/pre-islamic-mecca">Pre-Islamic Mecca</a></li></ul></section>
